2. AUDIT FINDINGS — what the prior session uncovered
The previous session's wrap-up message acknowledged these gaps. Treat them as hypotheses to verify, not conclusions to accept. Your multi-agent audit (step 3 below) should re-test each one and possibly find more.

CRITICAL / HIGH — actual goal violations
combat_lab/services/test_execution_service.py::run_headless bypasses run_battle — calls scenario.setup(engine) + raw engine.update() tick loop. Wired live via TestLabUIController.handle_run_headless (the Combat Lab UI's "Run Headless" button). The prior agent migrated test_executor.py::run_headless but missed this service-layer twin. Severity: HIGH — live production code path violates the unified-entry contract.

Battle Setup production path never calls build_manual_battle_spec — app.py::start_battle does fleet.battle.to_battle_ships(...) + inline BattleController construction. The Battle Setup spec compiler exists but is unused on the live path. Task 6.4 noted this as "deferred to Task 6.9" but Task 6.9's checklist closure ignored it. Severity: HIGH — the user-facing claim "every battle goes through run_battle(spec)" is false.

Visual-mode paths produce no BattleOutcome — BattleController drives BattleEngine directly; BattleResultsScreen reads from engine.ships not from an outcome. The unified-output goal is unmet for visual mode. Severity: HIGH — half the unified-flow contract.

Combat Lab discards BattleOutcome even when using run_battle — both combat_lab/runner.py and test_executor.py capture engine via a closure (engine_ref["engine"] = engine inside pre_tick_loop_callback) and call scenario._run_validation(engine) against the live engine. The BattleOutcome returned by run_battle is discarded. Severity: MEDIUM — circumvents the "outcome-only contract" goal.

BattleController.run_headless() is still a public method that runs full battles to completion through the controller. Contradicts the "thin per-frame tick driver" claim. Severity: HIGH if any caller still uses it; MEDIUM if dead but undeleted.

MEDIUM — silent behavior regressions or System Migration Policy violations
Strategic combat modifiers + storm shield interference emit placeholder effects the engine silently skips (documented in Phase 5.5 decisions but it IS a real battle-math regression). Pre-PROJ-269: _apply_shield_interference actually reduced ship shields in storm hexes; ShieldModifier/DamageModifier complexes actually shifted hit math. Post-PROJ-269: these modifiers appear in HitRecord.modifiers_applied for forensic trace but do NOT affect damage. This means strategy-layer storm hexes and shield/damage booster complexes have no gameplay effect in combat right now. Severity: MEDIUM-HIGH for gameplay; documented as out-of-scope for PROJ-269 itself.

Combat Lab scenario templates still ship legacy setup(battle_engine) methods marked "Legacy-compatible... retained for anything still driving setup() directly." This violates CLAUDE.md's System Migration Policy ("delete the old system entirely"). The legacy methods are now dead code on the production path. Severity: MEDIUM.

Spec fields declared but unused: AIPolicy (empty dataclass), CombatPolicies on TaskForceSpec/SquadronSpec (never read by the engine), ComponentStateSpec.is_active (read but never written by compilers), TaskForceOutcome carries only task_force_id (other promised fields per design.md missing). Severity: MEDIUM — design promises that don't pay off.

